Research Title: “A Comparative Study Of Successful Vs Unsuccessful Implementations Of Enterprise Systems”


Case Study 1: Successful Enterprise System Implementation

Company Overview (Why the enterprise system was implemented)
Company: San Miguel Corporation (SMC)
Industry: Food & Beverage, Packaging, Power, and Infrastructure
Reason for Implementation: San Miguel Corporation, one of the largest and most diversified conglomerates in the Philippines,
decided to implement an enterprise resource planning (ERP) system to integrate its complex operations across its various business units.
The aim was to streamline business processes, improve operational efficiency, 
and provide real-time data across departments, helping the company respond to market changes more swiftly.

Implementation Process
San Miguel Corporation adopted a phased approach to implement its ERP system. 
The company worked with an international ERP vendor, carefully planning each phase to align with the unique needs of its various divisions. 
The company also took time to provide extensive training for employees, ensuring they were familiar with the new system before full deployment. 
Pilot testing was conducted in smaller units, allowing for troubleshooting before full-scale integration.

Key Success Factors
The successful implementation of the ERP system at SMC can be attributed to several key factors:
- Strong leadership: Senior management's commitment to the project helped secure resources and align the entire organization around the ERP system.
- Clear objectives: SMC set specific goals for the implementation, such as reducing operational costs and improving supply chain management.
- User involvement: Employees were actively engaged in the process, with feedback incorporated into the system design and usage training.
- Thorough training: The company provided extensive training to its staff, ensuring that they could use the new system effectively.

Outcomes & Impact
The ERP implementation at San Miguel Corporation led to significant improvements in operational efficiency. 
Real-time access to data allowed the company to optimize supply chain management, reduce inventory costs, and improve coordination between departments. 
The company saw a reduction in operational bottlenecks, which ultimately led to enhanced productivity and a boost in profitability.

Case Study 2: Failed Enterprise System Implementation

Company Overview (Reason for implementing the enterprise system)
Company: Philippine Airlines (PAL)
Industry: Airline Services
Reason for Implementation: Philippine Airlines sought to implement an enterprise resource planning (ERP) system to centralize its operations, 
streamline its financial management, and improve customer service. The company was struggling with inefficient legacy systems and high operational costs, 
and it hoped that the new system would help improve service delivery and reduce overhead.

Implementation Process & Challenges
Philippine Airlines initially opted for a big-bang implementation strategy, attempting to switch over its entire system at once. Unfortunately, this led to several challenges. 
The implementation was rushed, and there was insufficient planning to account for the complexities of integrating the new system with PAL’s legacy infrastructure. 
User training was inadequate, leading to resistance among employees who were unfamiliar with the system. Additionally, there were numerous technical glitches and compatibility issues that slowed down the adoption process.

Key Failure Factors
The primary reasons for failure at Philippine Airlines included:
- Lack of proper planning: The company did not give enough time to prepare for the system's implementation, leading to confusion and disruption.
- Inadequate training: Many employees were not properly trained to use the new system, leading to a lack of user confidence.
- Poor communication: There was little communication between departments, resulting in misalignment of expectations and inefficiencies in the implementation process.
- Underestimating system complexity: The company did not fully account for the need for system customization, leading to technical issues during the rollout.

Outcomes & Consequences
The failed ERP implementation at Philippine Airlines resulted in widespread disruption. 
Operational delays became common, with the airline facing issues in booking management, finance tracking, and inventory control. 
The system's inability to integrate with existing processes led to inefficiencies, and the company experienced a sharp increase in operational costs. 
The lack of user adoption also led to a temporary decline in productivity and service quality, damaging PAL’s customer reputation. 
Ultimately, Philippine Airlines had to abandon the ERP system and revert to legacy systems, incurring financial losses in the process.


